From 0a8e1b68a810c4726c5aced589fc2469145fe90c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 14:09:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] Keep the surfaces on hide and unmap with a NULL buffer

hide() destroyed the layer surface, the popup, both wl_surfaces and their
viewports, and show() made them again, which costs a configure round trip
and four new shm buffers on every toggle of a 64 MiB handheld. The surfaces
are now created on the first show and live on. hide() attaches a NULL buffer
to the keyboard and the popup and commits; show() attaches the buffers
again. The protocol buffers, viewports and fractional-scale object are
untouched by a toggle.

Layer-shell and xdg-shell return an unmapped surface to the unconfigured
state: it is configured again only by a configure that answers a commit
without a buffer made after the unmap, and a buffer attached before that
is a protocol error. A configure that was already in flight when hide() ran
answers nothing, so it must not count. The decision when to map is a small
state (visible, configured, mapped, awaiting) in visibility.c without any
Wayland calls. A surface looks at a configure only while it is mapped or
after it asked for one; a leftover that arrives while hidden is neither
acked nor recorded. show() therefore commits without a buffer and waits for
the configure, except after a hide() that came before the first configure,
when nothing was unmapped and the pending answer is still the right one.
Its probe of the output size, which round-trips to the compositor, also
drains every leftover before the commit. A show() after a rotation, a size
or a scale change finds the stored geometry stale and renegotiates the same
way, and a configure with a new size resizes the buffers and drops the
popup role, which fixes its size when it is created. The probe goes
through a temporary layer surface, because the persistent surface cannot
learn about a rotation that happened while it was unmapped.

The key popup follows the same rules in the same state machine. While
show_popup is false the popup is never created: no wl_surface, viewport,
positioner or xdg_popup, and no buffers (kbd_popup_buffers allocates the
two ARGB buffers, twice the keyboard height, only when the popup is on and
frees them when it is turned off), so the 384 kB it costs at 240x100 are
really saved. When it is on, the role object is made once the keyboard is
mapped, a hide leaves it unconfigured and the next show commits it again
and waits, and xdg_popup.popup_done, sent when the compositor dismisses it,
destroys the role object so that the next show makes a new one. Touches no
longer wait for the popup to be configured, so a dismissed popup cannot
leave a keyboard that shows but ignores input.

--hidden, SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2 and SIGRTMIN, and the --auto activate and
deactivate callbacks go through the same show() and hide(). Toggling now
follows what the user asked for rather than the last configure, so a toggle
between show() and its configure hides again. Key release on hide and the
reset to the first layer on show are kept.

setup_buffer returned early with ds->width and height already updated, and
when mmap failed it left pool_data as MAP_FAILED after the old mapping had
been unmapped. Buffers are now built into temporaries and only then replace
the old ones, and drwsurf_resize builds both before replacing either, so a
failure leaves the old size, scale, mappings and cairo surfaces exactly as
they were. kbd_resize reports it and main restores the keyboard size. The
first resize has no old buffers: the surface then stays unusable, drawing
and attaching are skipped and the surface is not mapped until a later
configure succeeds. When only the popup fails it is dropped and skipped.

drwsurf_free releases the damage regions along with the buffers, which
drwsurf_drop_buffers keeps for a later resize, and main calls it on exit, so
that a leak checker has nothing of ours to report.

Tests: test-visibility covers the state machine (a configure in flight at
hide, show after hide asking for a configure, stale geometry, toggle
bursts, duplicate signals, auto activate and deactivate, the popup left
unconfigured by a hide, popup_done while hidden and while shown, a popup
that is off). test-popup replaces allocate_shm_file with a counter and
checks that a keyboard without popup allocates two buffers and none for the
popup, that turning it on makes them on demand and turning it off frees
them. test-buffer replaces allocate_shm_file to force an allocation
failure, an mmap failure and a failure of the second buffer, and checks
that size, scale, mappings and drawing are unchanged, that a retry works,
and that a surface without buffers skips every draw call. The tests free
what they allocate, so they are clean under the address and leak
sanitizers. Whether a compositor sends the configure a remap waits for,
accepts the NULL attach and the popup role rebuild, and keeps the exclusive
zone across a toggle needs a real compositor and is not covered.
